"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[9111],{798:function(e,i,n){n.d(i,{a:function(){return t}});function t(e){return{...e}}},9111:function(e,i,n){n.r(i),n.d(i,{default:function(){return a}});var t=n(7437),s=n(798);function o(e){let i={a:"a",em:"em",h2:"h2",h3:"h3",hr:"hr",li:"li",ol:"ol",p:"p",strong:"strong",ul:"ul",...(0,s.a)(),...e.components};return(0,t.jsxs)(t.Fragment,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(i.h2,{children:"Overview"}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["Carl Menger's ",(0,t.jsx)(i.em,{children:"Principles of Economics"})," (1871) is the founding text of the Austrian School of economics. In this groundbreaking work, Menger dismantles the classical labor theory of value and replaces it with the theory of subjective value -- the idea that the value of a good is not determined by the labor or materials that went into producing it, but by the importance an individual places on the satisfaction it provides. This single insight restructured the entire framework of economic reasoning and laid the groundwork for marginal utility analysis."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.p,{children:"Menger systematically builds from first principles: he defines what makes something an economic good, introduces the concept of orders of goods (consumer goods vs. producer goods), and develops his theory of marginal utility to explain how individuals make choices under scarcity. His analysis of the origin of money -- as an emergent institution arising from barter rather than government decree -- remains one of the most elegant explanations in all of economics."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.h2,{children:"Subjective Value vs. the Labor Theory of Value"}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["Before Menger, the dominant explanation for why things have value came from the classical economists -- Adam Smith, David Ricardo, and later Karl Marx. Their answer was the ",(0,t.jsx)(i.em,{children:"labor theory of value"}),": a good's value is determined by the amount of labor required to produce it. A diamond is expensive because it takes enormous effort to mine; a loaf of bread is cheap because it takes relatively little."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.p,{children:"Menger saw the fatal flaw in this reasoning. Consider water and diamonds. Water is essential for life, yet it is nearly free. Diamonds are decorative luxuries, yet they cost a fortune. If labor determines value, why doesn't the immense labor of building water infrastructure make tap water more expensive than diamonds? And if usefulness determines value, why isn't water -- the most useful substance on earth -- the most expensive?"}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["Menger's answer was radical in its simplicity: ",(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"value is not embedded in objects. Value exists only in the mind of the person making a choice."})," A glass of water is nearly worthless to someone sitting by a river. That same glass is worth a fortune to someone dying of thirst in a desert. The water hasn't changed. The person's circumstances have. This is the core of subjective value theory, and it resolved the so-called \"paradox of value\" that had plagued economics for a century."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.p,{children:'The implications are enormous. If value is subjective, then there is no such thing as an "objective" price for anything. Every exchange happens because both parties value what they receive more than what they give up. Trade is not zero-sum -- it is positive-sum, because both sides gain according to their own subjective valuations.'}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.h2,{children:"Marginal Utility: How Individuals Make Choices"}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["Menger didn't stop at saying value is subjective. He explained ",(0,t.jsx)(i.em,{children:"how"})," individuals assign value, and this is where marginal utility enters the picture."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.p,{children:"Imagine a farmer with five sacks of grain. He ranks them by the importance of the needs they satisfy:"}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.ol,{children:["\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.li,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"First sack"})," -- prevents starvation (survival)"]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.li,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"Second sack"})," -- provides enough nutrition for full health (strength)"]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.li,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"Third sack"})," -- feeds his livestock (meat and eggs)"]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.li,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"Fourth sack"})," -- distills into whiskey (luxury)"]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.li,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"Fifth sack"})," -- feeds pet parrots (amusement)"]}),"\n"]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["The value of any single sack is not determined by the most important use (survival) or the average of all uses. It is determined by the ",(0,t.jsx)(i.em,{children:"least important use"})," that would be sacrificed if one sack were lost. If the farmer loses one sack, he gives up feeding the parrots -- he doesn't starve. Therefore, the value of any one sack equals the marginal utility of the last, least-important use."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["This is the ",(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"law of diminishing marginal utility"}),": each additional unit of a good satisfies a less urgent need than the previous unit. It explains why prices fall as supply increases, why people pay less per unit when buying in bulk, and why even essential goods like water can be cheap when abundant."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.p,{children:'For investors, marginal thinking is foundational. The question is never "how valuable is this company in the abstract?" It\'s always "what is the next dollar of investment in this company worth, given what I already hold?"'}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.h2,{children:"Goods of Higher and Lower Order: The Theory of Imputation"}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["One of Menger's most original contributions is his classification of goods into ",(0,t.jsx)(i.em,{children:"orders"}),". ",(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"First-order goods"})," (also called consumer goods) satisfy human wants directly -- bread, clothing, shelter. ",(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"Higher-order goods"})," are the inputs used to produce first-order goods -- flour, ovens, and baker's labor are second-order goods; wheat fields, farming equipment, and seed are third-order goods; and so on."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["The critical insight is the direction of value assignment. Classical economists assumed value flowed forward through the production chain: labor and materials had inherent value, and that value was passed into the final product. Menger reversed this entirely. ",(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"Value flows backward -- from the consumer good to the producer goods."})," Flour is valuable only because bread is valuable. Iron ore is valuable only because steel is valuable, and steel is valuable only because the things made of steel are valuable to consumers."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["This is the ",(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"theory of imputation"}),": the value of higher-order goods is ",(0,t.jsx)(i.em,{children:"imputed"})," from the value of the lower-order goods they produce. This has direct applications in modern finance:"]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.ul,{children:["\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.li,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"Asset valuation"})," works the same way. A factory is valuable because of the products it produces, not because of the steel and concrete in its walls."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.li,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"Capital allocation"})," decisions require understanding which higher-order investments will produce the most valued consumer outputs."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.li,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"Startup valuation"})," depends entirely on imputed future consumer demand, not on the cost of the inputs (code, labor, servers) consumed so far."]}),"\n"]}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.h3,{children:"Time and Uncertainty in Production"}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["Menger emphasized that production takes time, and that higher-order goods must be combined in the right proportions and at the right time to yield their intended output. This introduces genuine uncertainty into economic activity. An entrepreneur commits capital to higher-order goods ",(0,t.jsx)(i.em,{children:"now"}),", based on expectations about consumer demand ",(0,t.jsx)(i.em,{children:"later"}),". If those expectations are wrong, the invested capital is wasted."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.p,{children:"This framework is the foundation of Austrian capital theory and business cycle analysis. Malinvestment -- the misallocation of capital to higher-order goods that ultimately don't serve consumer demand -- is the Austrian explanation for recessions. It's also a precise description of what happens when a venture-backed startup burns through hundreds of millions building a product nobody wants."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.h2,{children:"The Origin of Money"}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["Chapter VIII of the ",(0,t.jsx)(i.em,{children:"Principles"})," contains Menger's theory of the origin of money, which is both elegant and counterintuitive. Money was not invented by a government or decreed into existence by a ruler. It emerged spontaneously from the problem of barter."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["The problem with barter is the ",(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"double coincidence of wants"}),": if you have fish and want shoes, you need to find someone who has shoes and wants fish. In any economy with more than a handful of goods, this is prohibitively difficult. Menger showed that certain commodities -- because of their durability, divisibility, portability, and broad desirability -- naturally became more ",(0,t.jsx)(i.em,{children:"marketable"})," than others. That is, they were easier to trade away in future exchanges."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["Over time, the most marketable commodity in any region became accepted as a ",(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"medium of exchange"})," -- not because anyone declared it money, but because individuals rationally chose to accept it, knowing they could trade it later. Gold and silver became money in most civilizations not by decree but because they possessed the physical properties that made them the most marketable commodities available."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.p,{children:["This analysis matters well beyond monetary history. Menger demonstrated that complex institutions can emerge from individual action without centralized planning. Markets, prices, legal customs, and money itself are examples of what Friedrich Hayek (Menger's intellectual heir) later called ",(0,t.jsx)(i.strong,{children:"spontaneous order"}),". Understanding this principle helps explain why attempts to centrally plan complex economic systems -- from Soviet five-year plans to algorithmic stablecoin designs -- tend to fail in ways their designers never anticipated."]}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.h2,{children:"Why This Matters"}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.p,{children:"Menger's framework is essential for any investor or analyst who wants to understand pricing at a foundational level. Subjective value theory underpins modern demand analysis, and his insights on the spontaneous emergence of institutions (money, markets, prices) provide a powerful lens for understanding why financial markets organize the way they do. His work is the entry point into Austrian economics, which offers a distinct and often contrarian perspective on credit cycles, capital allocation, and entrepreneurial judgment."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.p,{children:"The theory of imputation -- value flowing from consumer to producer goods -- is how every discounted cash flow model implicitly works. You value a company based on what its outputs will be worth to consumers, discounted back to the present. Menger formalized this logic 150 years ago."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.p,{children:"His emphasis on uncertainty and time in production is the intellectual ancestor of modern real options theory and venture capital methodology. Every capital allocation decision is, at its core, a Mengerian bet: committing resources to higher-order goods today in the hope that they will produce valued consumer goods tomorrow."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.h2,{children:"Key Takeaways"}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.ul,{children:["\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.li,{children:"Value is subjective: goods have value because individuals judge them useful for satisfying wants, not because of embedded labor costs."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.li,{children:"Marginal utility explains pricing: the value of additional units of a good declines as quantity increases, and the value of any unit equals the utility of the least important use it serves."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.li,{children:"Goods exist in orders: first-order goods satisfy wants directly; higher-order goods (capital, raw materials) derive their value from their contribution to producing first-order goods."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.li,{children:"Value flows backward through the production chain (imputation), not forward from inputs to outputs -- this is how DCF models work."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.li,{children:"Money emerged spontaneously from barter as the most marketable commodity, not by government fiat."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.li,{children:"Production takes time and involves genuine uncertainty -- entrepreneurs bear the risk of committing capital to higher-order goods before consumer demand is known."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.li,{children:"Complex economic institutions (money, prices, markets) can arise from individual action without central planning -- the principle of spontaneous order."}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.li,{children:"Economic reasoning must start from the actions and valuations of individuals -- methodological individualism."}),"\n"]}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.h2,{children:"Further Reading"}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.ul,{children:["\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.li,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(i.a,{href:"/read/stigler-theory-of-price",children:"Stigler's Theory of Price"})," -- the Chicago School's rigorous treatment of how subjective valuations aggregate into market prices"]}),"\n",(0,t.jsxs)(i.li,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(i.a,{href:"/read/business-history-of-finance",children:"Business History of Finance"})," -- how the institutions Menger described (money, markets, firms) actually evolved historically"]}),"\n"]}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.hr,{}),"\n",(0,t.jsx)(i.p,{children:(0,t.jsxs)(i.em,{children:["This is a living document. Contributions welcome via ",(0,t.jsx)(i.a,{href:"https://github.com/NickNemo17/wyandanch-library",children:"GitHub"}),"."]})})]})}function a(){let e=arguments.length>0&&void 0!==arguments[0]?arguments[0]:{},{wrapper:i}={...(0,s.a)(),...e.components};return i?(0,t.jsx)(i,{...e,children:(0,t.jsx)(o,{...e})}):o(e)}}}]);